@742021 = ~Panacea (Necromancy)
Level: 4
Range: visual range of the caster
Duration: instantaneous
Casting Time: 3
Area of Effect: one creature
Saving Throw: none

This spell cures ailments affecting the recipient, whether natural or magical. When this spell is cast, it immediately neutralizes effects of poison, disease, fever and nausea, and restores 1d10 hit points.

All allies within 5 meters of the recipient also benefit from reduced effects of the spells:
they recover 1D6 hit points and are cured of fever and nausea, but poison and disease are cured in only 70% of cases (independently of each other).~

@7421 = ~Rage~
@7422 = ~Rage (Enchantment)
Level: 4
Range: 0
Duration: 1 turn
Casting Time: 4
Area of Effect: caster
Saving Throw: none

This spell sends the warlord into a berserker-like rage.
While under Rage, the warlord gains +2 to attack and damage rolls and 15 temporary hit points. He is immune to fear, charm, confusion, hold, stun and sleep effects, as well as Maze, Imprisonment and Symbol spells.
The spell has several disadvantages, however. Rage drives the warlord to attack constantly, preventing him from concentrating enough to cast spells. In addition, this relentless aggression weakens his defenses, imposing a -2 penalty to AC.
Each round also has a 20% chance of triggering a save vs. spell. Failure sends the warlord into a berserk frenzy: unable to distinguish friend from foe, he attacks the nearest creature until it dies, then moves on to the next until the spell ends.
At level 16, the experienced warlord may choose to enter into a higher form of rage, Blood Rage. He then gets other bonuses in addition to those already provided by Rage: +3 to Strength, +1 to Constitution, +2 to Movement Speed, +1 attack per round. But Blood Rage has two major disadvantages: first, he can re-enter into Berserk Fury every round if he fails a save vs. spell. Second, the physical strain (muscle tension, elevated blood pressure, excess adrenaline, and similar effects) deals 1D3 points of damage per round.~
@74221 = ~Blood Rage~
@74222 = ~Rage (Enchantment)
Level: 4
Range: 0
Duration: 1 turn
Casting Time: 2
Area of Effect: caster
Saving Throw: none

This spell allows the warlord to enter into a state of rage that he must choose while casting the spell.
Rage sends the warlord into a berserker-like rage.
While under Rage, the warlord gains +2 to attack and damage rolls and 15 temporary hit points. He is immune to fear, charm, confusion, hold, stun and sleep effects, as well as Maze, Imprisonment and Symbol spells.
The spell has several disadvantages, however. Rage drives the warlord to attack constantly, preventing him from concentrating enough to cast spells. In addition, this relentless aggression weakens his defenses, imposing a -2 penalty to AC.
Each round also has a 20% chance of triggering a save vs. spell. Failure sends the warlord into a berserk frenzy: unable to distinguish friend from foe, he attacks the nearest creature until it dies, then moves on to the next until the spell ends.

Blood Rage is a major form of rage, that makes him get other bonuses in addition to those already provided by Rage: +3 to Strength, +1 to Constitution, +2 to Movement Speed, +1 attack per round. But Blood Rage has two major disadvantages: first, he can re-enter into Berserk Fury every round if he fails a save vs. spell. Second, the physical strain (muscle tension, elevated blood pressure, excess adrenaline, and similar effects) deals 1D3 points of damage per round.~
@74223 = ~Rage (Enchantment)
Level: 4
Range: 0
Duration: 1 turn
Casting Time: 4
Area of Effect: caster
Saving Throw: none

This spell allows the warlord to enter into a state of Blood Rage.
While under Blood Rage he gets +3 to Strength, +1 to Constitution, +2 to movement speed, +1 attack per round and +2 to hit and damage. He is also immune to fear, charm, confusion, hold, stun and sleep effects, as well as Maze, Imprisonment and Symbol spells. Finally, he gets 15 temporary extra hit points.

The spell has a disadvantage, however. Rage drives the warlord to attack constantly, preventing him from concentrating enough to cast spells.~
